              "image_path": "images/page_11_table_5.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>2</td><td>7</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>5</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>3</td><td>8</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.231,
            0.77,
            0.249
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Magic squares were studied in different parts of"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.244,
            0.249,
            0.846,
            0.286
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "the world at different points of time including India, Japan, Central Asia, and Europe."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.245,
            0.286,
            0.847,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Indian mathematicians have worked extensively on magic squares, describing general methods of constructing them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.322,
            0.848,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The work of Indian mathematicians was not just limited to 3 × 3 and 4 × 4 grids, which we considered above, but also extended to 5 × 5 and other larger square grids. We shall learn more about these in later grades."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.396,
            0.746,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The occurrence of magic squares is not limited to scholarly mathematical works. They are found in many places in India. The picture to the right is of a 3 × 3 magic square found on a pillar in a temple in Palani, Tamil Nadu. The temple dates back to the 8th century CE."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.488,
            0.744,
            0.526
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3 × 3 magic squares can also be found in homes and shops in India. The Navagraha Yantra is one such example shown below."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
